  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Enhancing Community-Event Based Surveillance in Point of Entry and Migration Corridors to Inform Preparedness Plans in Urban Areas of Zimbabwe</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The relationship between population mobility and health is dynamic and complex. Population movements impact public health as mobile populations interact with host communities within and across borders, particularly in Zimbabwe considering the porosity of the borders with neighbouring countries and the high volume of cross-border movements of goods and people, through irregular/unofficial crossing points. The transmission of communicable diseases is a critical dimension of population mobility and health. Urban centres in Zimbabwe are host to both internal and international migrants, as well as Zimbabwe returnees, posing unique challenges for the health system. 
 
This project seeks to strengthen IOM&#x2019;s Flow Monitoring (FM) activities along major migration corridors and urban centres, including Points of Entry (PoEs), border communities and congregation areas. Through a more comprehensive picture of mobility dynamics, as well as to contribute to strengthen health security systems through community event-based surveillance, informing epidemic preparedness, and triggering immediate and effective response in the event of a health crisis or emergency in targeted areas of Zimbabwe.</narrative>
  </description>
